# NEVER GUESS (MANDATORY)

**ENFORCEMENT**: A guess presented as a fix is a defect, worse than no fix at all — it consumes
the user's verification effort and can bury the real cause under new symptoms.

## The rule

Never state a cause, or ship a change premised on one, that you have not verified against a
primary source: the actual code, the actual API response, the actual rendered output, the actual
test failure, the actual log.

If you cannot verify it, you MUST say so explicitly and either verify it or stop.

## What counts as a guess

- "This is probably because…" / "SwiftUI likely…" / "That API tends to…" — plausible mechanism,
  unread source. Read the source.
- Believing a code comment or doc-string that asserts runtime behavior, without confirming the
  behavior still holds. Comments describe the author's intent at write time, not current reality.
- Believing a previous agent's (or your own earlier) claim about why something worked. Re-derive it.
- Attributing a symptom to the nearest recent change without tracing the mechanism end to end.
- Framework/library behavior recalled from training rather than checked in this version.

## Mandatory disclosure

When something is unverified and you proceed anyway, label it inline, in the user-facing text,
before the conclusion — not in a footnote:

> Unverified: I have not confirmed X. If X does not hold, this fix is wrong.

Never launder a hypothesis into a claim by dropping the hedge in the summary. If the body said
"probably", the summary MUST NOT say "the cause was".

## The two-strike stop

If a fix for a given symptom fails **twice**, STOP patching. A second failure means the model of
the system is wrong, not that the patch was slightly off. Do not attempt a third variation.
Instead: state what the failures prove is false, name what you would have to observe to know the
real cause, and get that observation before editing again.

Chasing symptoms one at a time after a wrong root-cause call is the single most expensive failure
mode in this repository's history. Un-diagnose before re-fixing.

## Tests are not verification of UI or behavior

A green test suite proves only what the tests assert. It is NOT evidence that a rendered screen
looks right, that navigation feels right, or that a live endpoint returns what you assumed.
Never report a UI or integration change as working on the strength of passing tests alone —
either observe the real output (screenshot the running app, read the live response), or hand it
back explicitly labelled as unverified.

## Verification precedence

When sources disagree, trust in this order — higher always wins:

1. Observed runtime behavior (screenshot, log, live response, actual run)
2. Source code as it exists right now
3. Tests that actually execute the path in question
4. Documentation, comments, memory-bank entries, prior agent reports, training recall

Never resolve a conflict in favor of a lower tier. When a lower tier turns out to be wrong,
fix it as part of the change rather than leaving it to mislead the next agent.
